{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And behold, the staff of Aharon for the house of Levi had sprouted.</b> For it is the staff of the house of Levi." "<b>And it produced blossoms and shot out a bud and ripened almonds.</b> The reason it ripened almonds and no other fruit is that this is an esteemed fruit, and it is swifter to emerge from year to year more than all other fruits; and because of this they are called שקדים (almonds) from the language of שקידה (diligence/watchfulness), as it is written (Jeremiah 1:12): ״כי שוקד אני על דברי לעשותו״ — 'for I am watchful over My word to perform it.' And this fruit is bitter from its beginning. And likewise the expression of almonds is always mentioned in connection with the attribute of judgment: ״ויגמול שקדים״ — 'and it ripened almonds'; ״מקל שקד אני רואה״ — 'a staff of almond I see' (Jeremiah 1:11) — that is, a branch with the almonds that are upon it, which are bitter, alluding to calamity and to the bitterness of the attribute of judgment stretched out in the destruction of the Temple by the hand of Nevukhadnetztzar; for he said likewise (Jeremiah 1:13): ״סיר נפוח אני רואה ופניו מפני צפונה״, and likewise it is written explicitly (Daniel 9:14): ״וישקד ה׳ על הרעה״. If so, this staff that ripened almonds — from the name of the fruit and its swiftness and its taste we learn that whoever contests the priesthood, the Holy One, blessed be He, is swift to exact retribution from him and stretches out against him the attribute of judgment, as we find with King Uziyahu, who sent his hand to offer incense in the place of the priest, and it is written of him (II Chronicles 26:19): ״והצרעת זרחה במצחו״."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:33:40.274985980Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}